<ul>
<li>The field around the nearest neighbour. Several literatures attack this same cost, and they
split cleanly into two kinds. The split is the point.
[pause]</li>
<li>Top group reduces the SAME object we do. Every one needs a certificate — some statement that
deleting is safe.</li>
<li>Column generation certifies a column can never enter an optimal solution. A statement about an
argmax.</li>
<li>Sampling removes alternatives carrying real probability and corrects asymptotically.</li>
<li>Both exact for something. Neither exact for a sum.
[pause]</li>
<li>Reachability is the third, and its certificate is a feasibility constraint. Only one of the
three that still holds when the objective stops being a maximum and becomes a sum.
[pause]</li>
<li>Bottom group does something different, and I don't want to misrepresent it.</li>
<li>Perturbed utility doesn't reduce this computation. It changes what's computed. PURC has the
traveller choose a flow vector over the entire network under conservation. No choice set to
generate at all, estimation is linear regression. Genuinely different, and elegant.</li>
<li>What it isn't, yet, is a scheduled day. Route choice for one trip, no activity durations, no
mandatory agenda.
[pause]</li>
<li>Yao and Zhang's Markovian version is closer — same MDP — and kills the bi-level estimation by
inverting the map from Q-values to policy. Price: the policy has to be recoverable at each
state. Our data is about a quarter of an observation per state. Their demonstration is a small
grid. Different data regimes. I won't claim a win.
[pause]</li>
<li>So the honest statement isn't that we're faster or they're wrong. A constraint is the only
certificate that survives the move from a maximum to a sum. That's why reachability is the
reduction available here.</li>
</ul>
